/* book Worked example: Q: For A ⇌ 2B, K = 0.222 at 400 K. A feed of 1 mol A is at 400 K. (a) Find XA at P = 1 bar, 0.5 bar and 2 bar. (b) Find XA at 1 bar if 1 mol inert is also fed.
   1. Let ξ be the extent in mol. With 1 mol A initially and no inert: nA = 1 − ξ, nB = 2ξ, ntotal = 1 + ξ. Conversion of A is XA = ξ/1 = ξ.
   2. Mole fractions are yA = (1 − ξ)/(1 + ξ) and yB = 2ξ/(1 + ξ). For A ⇌ 2B, Σν = 2 − 1 = 1. With P° = 1 bar, K = (P/P°) × 4ξ²/(1 − ξ²).
   3. At P = 1 bar: 0.222 = 4ξ²/(1 − ξ²). This gives 0.222 = 4.222ξ², ξ² = 0.05258, ξ = 0.2293. So XA = 0.229.
   4. At P = 0.5 bar: 0.222 = 0.5 × 4ξ²/(1 − ξ²) = 2ξ²/(1 − ξ²). This gives 0.222 = 2.222ξ², ξ² = 0.09991, ξ = 0.3161. So XA = 0.316.
   5. At P = 2 bar: 0.222 = 2 × 4ξ²/(1 − ξ²) = 8ξ²/(1 − ξ²). This gives 0.222 = 8.222ξ², ξ² = 0.02700, ξ = 0.1643. So XA = 0.164.
   6. With 1 mol inert at 1 bar: ntotal = 2 + ξ, yA = (1 − ξ)/(2 + ξ), yB = 2ξ/(2 + ξ). K = 4ξ²/[(2 + ξ)(1 − ξ)]. Substituting K = 0.222 gives 0.222(2 − ξ − ξ²) = 4ξ², which rearranges to 4.222ξ² + 0.222ξ − 0.444 = 0.
   7. The positive root is ξ = [−0.222 + √(0.222² + 4 × 4.222 × 0.444)]/(2 × 4.222). The square root is 2.7473, so ξ = 2.5253/8.444 = 0.2991. Thus XA = 0.299.
   Answer: At 0.5 bar XA = 0.316; at 1 bar XA = 0.229; at 2 bar XA = 0.164; with 1 mol inert at 1 bar XA = 0.299. */
