/* book Worked example 2: Q: Ammonia synthesis, N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃, has K = 0.100 at 500 K. A stoichiometric feed (1 mol N₂, 3 mol H₂) reaches equilibrium as an ideal gas. Find the conversion of N₂ at 1 bar and at 100 bar, and at 100 bar when the feed also carries 0.444 mol argon (10 mol % of the feed).
   1. Moles: nN₂ = 1 − ξ, nH₂ = 3(1 − ξ), nNH₃ = 2ξ, nT = 4 − 2ξ; Σν = −2. Then K = [yNH₃²/(yN₂ yH₂³)](P/P°)−2 = 16ξ²(2 − ξ)²/[27(1 − ξ)⁴] × (P°/P)².
   2. At 1 bar: 16ξ²(2 − ξ)²/[27(1 − ξ)⁴] = 0.100. Trial ξ = 0.15 gives 0.0874 and ξ = 0.16 gives 0.1032, so ξ = 0.158: X = 15.8 %, yNH₃ = 0.316/3.684 = 0.086.
   3. At 100 bar the same left side must equal 0.100 × 100² = 1000. Trial ξ = 0.84 gives 859 and ξ = 0.85 gives 1118, so ξ = 0.846: X = 84.6 %, yNH₃ = 1.692/2.308 = 0.733.
   4. With 0.444 mol argon at 100 bar, nT = 4.444 − 2ξ, so the factor (4 − 2ξ)² becomes (4.444 − 2ξ)². Solving gives ξ = 0.832: X = 83.2 % and yNH₃ = 0.599.
   5. K is 0.100 in every case; only the split between the mole fractions and (P/P°)−2 changes.
   Answer: Raising the pressure from 1 to 100 bar lifts the N₂ conversion from 15.8 % to 84.6 % because Σν = −2; 10 mol % argon in the feed at 100 bar lowers it to 83.2 %. */
