/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - At a few bar a gas is ideal and the fugacity of species i is its partial pressure, fᵢ = yᵢP.
   - K is the equilibrium constant at temperature T (dimensionless); φᵢ is the fugacity coefficient of species i in the mixture; yᵢ is its gas mole fraction; P is total pressure in bar; P° = 1 bar is standard pressure; νᵢ is the stoichiometric c
   - For an ideal gas every φᵢ = 1, giving K = Π(yᵢP/P°)νᵢ.
   - The loop is unavoidable: φᵢ depends on the yᵢ you seek.
   - The standard state of a pure solid or pure liquid is the pure condensed phase at system T and P.
   - Limestone calcination, CaCO₃(s) ⇌ CaO(s) + CO₂(g): both solids have activity 1, so K = aCO₂ = yCO₂P/P°.
   - Carbon behaves the same way: C(s) + CO₂(g) ⇌ 2CO(g) has K = (yCO P/P°)²/(yCO₂P/P°), since aC = 1.
   - for a reacting liquid mixture: γᵢ is the activity coefficient of species i, xᵢ its liquid mole fraction, νᵢ its stoichiometric coefficient; the product runs over liquid species.
   - Activity 1 belongs to a pure phase only.
   - Real feeds crack, reform and shift together.
*/
