/* book exam traps: Putting a pure solid into K as a mole fraction. Its activity is 1, so CaCO₃ and CaO never appear in the calcination constant — only yCO₂P/P° does. | Using one extent for two reactions. Each independent reaction needs its own extent and equilibrium equation. | Treating φᵢ as a pure-species constant. It depends on composition, so high-pressure equilibrium must be iterated. | Counting reactions from the feed list. Take the rank of the atom matrix; a reaction that is a sum of the others adds no extent. | Dropping standard pressure. In K = Π(yᵢP/P°)νᵢ pressure enters as dimensionless P/P°, so P and P° need the same units. */
